{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9vyETVRvH9DPPNJKxfL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `Hijack` in `response_writer.go`. It used to refuse to hijack as soon as the headers had been sent. That broke websocket libraries that send the `101 Switching Protocols` header first and hijack after. Now it only refuses once body bytes have been written (`w.size > 0`). If nothing has been written yet, hijacking still works and marks the response as written.\n\n- I changed the error message to `gin: response body already written`. The `errHijackAlreadyWritten` name stays the same, so anyone comparing against that error is unaffected.\n- I added a test case to `response_writer_test.go` for hijacking after only the header has been sent. `go test ./...` passes.\n\nI haven't committed anything, because you're on a detached HEAD. I can create a branch and commit it as `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle` if you want. One thing to know: handlers that relied on the old error when hijacking after only headers were flushed will now hijack successfully instead."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":176,"cache_read_input_tokens":34368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":176,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"acbe72b8-ffb8-40e8-8af5-47bbecbfd935","uuid":"97c496dc-61ba-42a8-a174-82e61f934b98","timestamp":"2026-10-18T15:22:27.215Z","request_id":"req_011Cg9vyEFar7STbh7MvfScA"}
